Text Te13
Description: A Frame Node embeds a table or graph in a diagram instead of showing it as a separate window. It is especially useful for the Web application style with its tab user interface.  A frame node is just a kind of text node. Its rectangular outline (size and location) specify where the table or graph appears within a diagram -- usually on the right or lower section of the diagram window. ~
~
For an input defined as an edit table, a user can enter values into the  table in the frame node. For an output, it displays the result as a table or graph in the frame node, using the view and options as the result was last saved in desktop Analytica.  When creating a model to be viewed in ACP, it's a good idea to go through each result and check that it shows the view and options that you want before uploading it to ACP.~
~
HOW TO MAKE A FRAME NODE~
1.  Create a text node in the diagram. Position and size the node where you want to view the result. You may create more than one frame nodes. There's no need to add text (description) to these text nodes.~
~
2. For each node you want to make into a Frame node, ~
   a. select its parent module from the menu below. ~
   b. select the text node from the menu below that. ~
   c. Check "Use as Frame node"~
~
3. Finally select style options for the Frame node. Move your cursor over each item for a description of what it does, and ~
In the control panel below, select the module where the text node is located from the 'Select module' listing.  Select the frame node's identifier from the 'Select frame node' listing.  Then check "Use as a module frame node".
